{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q020_syntaxin_fragment_reclassification","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts (stable release 2.08; the latest periodic update 2.08-2026-08-16 returns the same 1dn1 record). All syntaxin 1A N-terminal structures named in the question sit in one lineage: class a (all alpha), fold a.47 'STAT-like' (sunid 47654; fold comment: '4 long helices; bundle, left-handed twist (coiled coil); right-handed superhelix'), superfamily a.47.2 't-snare proteins' (47661, which has a single family), family a.47.2.1 (47662). Domains: d1br0a_ (17772), d1ez3a_/d1ez3b_/d1ez3c_ (17768/17769/17770) and d3c98b_ (173092) are under the curated protein node 'Syntaxin 1A N-terminal domain' (47663), species rat (47664). d1dn1b_ is also a.47.2.1 but in 2.08 it is sunid 302344 under the protein node 'automated matches' (191190), species 'Rattus norvegicus' (311131), with the comment 'automated match to d3c98b_'; in release 1.75 the same SID was a curated domain (sunid 17771) directly under protein node 47663, and sunid 17771 no longer resolves in 2.08. 1dn1 itself is an obsolete PDB entry (obsolete 2008-03-25, replaced by 3c98, 'Revised structure of the munc18a-syntaxin1 complex'). The fragment domains d1br0a_ and all three 1ez3 domains carry the curator comment 'three-helical fragment; similar to one spectrin repeat' and, in 2.08, a machine-readable heterogeneity flag 'ms' ('missing some secondary structures that made up less than one-third of the common domain'). In 1.75 the comment was present (1br0 additionally 'mutant') but the ms flag was absent, so the explicit fragment annotation was added between 1.75 and 2.08; SCOPe does not record the rationale. Observed ATOM sequences: d1br0a_ 120 residues, d1ez3a_ 124, d1dn1b_ 222; the 1dn1 B sequence begins with the identical Habc segment seen in 1br0/1ez3 and continues roughly 100 residues C-terminally, so the 'full domain' in SCOPe's sense is the whole chain including the segment C-terminal to Habc. d3c98b_ and d1dn1b_ carry no fragment or heterogeneity flags. Quality (retrieved for both): 1dn1 SPACI/AEROSPACI 0.2548, 2.6 Å, R 0.239; 3c98 0.3124, 2.6 Å, R 0.208.

Interpretation of what this does to fragment-based fold assignments. (1) SCOPe assigns a fragment the fold of the complete domain it belongs to, not the fold its own coordinates would suggest. On their own, 1br0 and 1ez3 are three-helix bundles, and the curators explicitly note they resemble one spectrin repeat; yet they are placed in the four-helix STAT-like fold a.47 with an 'ms' flag rather than in a three-helical fold. The spectrin repeat is classified in a different fold entirely (a.7 'Spectrin repeat-like', superfamily a.7.1, family a.7.1.1), and the a.47 fold node carries SCOPe's explicit warning that levels above superfamily do not imply homology. So the fragment's resemblance to a spectrin repeat is a structural observation only: it is not a SCOPe homology claim, and the fragment structures must not be used to argue that the syntaxin Habc domain is evolutionarily related to spectrin. (2) The 'ms' flag means the fragment domains are incomplete representatives of their own fold: a fold-level or FATCAT-based comparison that uses d1br0a_ or a 1ez3 domain as the query or template will sample fewer than the fold's four core helices and can produce misleading matches to three-helical folds. For benchmarking or template selection, these domains should be excluded or stratified as annotated heterogeneous cases, as the skill's special-annotation guidance requires. (3) The complete-domain reference is now d3c98b_, not 1dn1 chain B. 1dn1 is obsolete, and in 2.08 its chain B is only an automated match to d3c98b_ under an 'automated matches' protein node; its lineage is inherited from 3c98 rather than independently curated. Automated status is not low quality, but for a curated representative of the complete syntaxin 1A N-terminal domain the appropriate choice is d3c98b_, which is in the curated protein node and carries no fragment flag. The retrieved quality fields show 3c98 with a higher SPACI and lower R factor at equal resolution; these are technical-quality values for representative selection, not evidence about homology or function. (4) The relationship among all these domains is at the family level (a.47.2.1), the strongest SCOPe relationship, consistent with their being the same protein from the same species; the superfamily a.47.2 additionally links them to other t-SNARE N-terminal domains such as the Sso1 protein node (47665), which supports probable common ancestry but not identical function or regulatory behavior.

Limits. SCOPe does not state why the ms annotation was added or which secondary structures are counted as missing; that would need the SCOPe curation notes or the primary literature. SCOPe records nothing about conformational state: whether the extra C-terminal helices in 1dn1/3c98 chain B reflect the Munc18-bound 'closed' conformation, and whether the Habc fragment alone is biologically autonomous, are literature questions, not SCOPe facts. The classification of 1dn1 chain A (d1dn1a_, e.25.1.1, Sec1/munc18-like, automated match to d3c98a_) says nothing about the syntaxin domain's boundaries. No residue-level alignment between the fragments and 3c98/1dn1 chain B was inspected beyond the retrieved ATOM sequences, so the exact residue range of the 'missing' elements is not verified here. Finally, placing a fragment and the complete domain in the same family is a statement about the protein they come from, not evidence that a three-helix fragment alone would be classifiable into a.47 by structure comparison; fragment-based fold assignments should be read as inherited from the complete homolog and preserved with their heterogeneity caveat.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__get_scop_pdb_entry","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","mcp__scope__get_scop_sequence","mcp__scope__resolve_scop_identifier","mcp__scope__compare_structure_quality","mcp__scope__search_scop","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1br0","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1br0/chains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1br0/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ez3","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ez3/chains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ez3/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1dn1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1dn1/chains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1dn1/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/3c98","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/3c98/chains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/3c98/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1br0a_","/api/v1/re